window.PREP_DATA = window.PREP_DATA || {};
window.PREP_DATA.devops = {
  notes: [
    { title: "DevOps idea", body: "The problem before\nBuilders throw a finished app over a wall and walk away. Release week is a surprise. Tools pile up with no shared owner. You list Jenkins and Kubernetes and stop.\n\nWhat this is\nDevOps is a way of working, not only a job title. The people who write the app and the people who run it share one loop: build, test, ship, watch production, and learn. Automation makes that loop fast and repeatable. Tools help, but a pile of YAML without shared ownership is not DevOps.\n\nWhat it solves\nYou ship often without surprising users. Feedback is a loop, not a ceremony. Tell the story from commit to rollback, not only the tool names.\n\nReal-life example\nOne kitchen, not a cook who plates and a waiter who never talks. The ticket goes cook, taste, serve, watch the table, change the recipe. A stack of recipe cards with nobody owning the floor is not a restaurant.\n\nUses\nWhat is DevOps interviews. Opening any delivery story.\n\nWatch out\nNaming twenty tools with no loop. Claiming DevOps because you have a YAML file. Culture talk with zero automation, or robots with zero shared on-call." },
    { title: "CI", body: "The problem before\nPeople merge once a month. Tests run only on one laptop. Bugs show up at release week. A slow red job trains everyone to ignore the robot.\n\nWhat this is\nCI means Continuous Integration. People merge small changes often, then a robot lints, tests, builds, and scans. Feedback should arrive in minutes, not at release week. The same checks should work on your laptop and in the pipeline.\n\nWhat it solves\nIntegration pain stays small. Fast, honest tests are the point. If CI is slow or flaky, people stop trusting it, so keep the job short and deterministic.\n\nReal-life example\nAirport security for every suitcase, not a search on launch day. Tasting the soup on two stoves if versions matter. A smoke alarm that beeps for toast soon has no believers.\n\nUses\nEvery pull request. Interviews: What is CI, required checks, flaky tests.\n\nWatch out\nDeploying when tests are red. Node latest in CI. Retrying a red test until green and calling that a fix." },
    { title: "CD", body: "The problem before\nMain is a junk drawer. You rebuild for prod and the bytes differ from staging. People say we do CD and mean two different things.\n\nWhat this is\nCD means the main branch stays releasable. Continuous Delivery means you could ship any green build. Continuous Deployment means every green main actually goes live; people mix the letters, so define them. Prod may still need an approval, and you promote the same artifact, not a fresh rebuild.\n\nWhat it solves\nWhat you tested is what you ship. Config and secrets may change between rooms; the image digest should not. Delivery packs the suitcase, and deployment sends the taxi.\n\nReal-life example\nA boxed lunch with a fingerprint on the lid. You carry that same box from the rehearsal kitchen to the dining room. You do not recook between rooms and still call it the same meal.\n\nUses\nRelease interviews. Promotion vs rebuild. Delivery vs deployment.\n\nWatch out\nTagging latest in staging and latest in prod. Rebuilding for prod because compile flags felt different. Saying CD without saying which meaning." },
    { title: "Pipeline as code", body: "The problem before\nThe real jobs live only in a web UI. Nobody can grep history. A production check changes and the PR never saw it.\n\nWhat this is\nPipeline as code means the CI/CD steps live in git next to the app. GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, and Jenkinsfile are common forms. You review pipeline changes like app changes, and history tells you who changed a check. Keep the workflow file in the same pull request as the code it tests.\n\nWhat it solves\nThe robot recipe is reviewable. Branches get the same checks. A click-only pipeline stops being a secret in someone's account.\n\nReal-life example\nThe kitchen checklist hangs on the same clipboard as the dish, not in the manager's private notebook. When the checklist changes, the same review tastes both.\n\nUses\nGitHub Actions workflows. Jenkinsfile. Any CI you want to audit.\n\nWatch out\nEditing production jobs only in a GUI. Pasting cloud keys into the YAML. A required check name that does not match the job name." },
    { title: "IaC", body: "The problem before\nProd buckets appear from clicks. The next plan wants to destroy a surprise. Two people apply at once and the catalog lies.\n\nWhat this is\nIaC means Infrastructure as Code: you describe servers, networks, and buckets in files, and a tool makes the real cloud match. Terraform, Pulumi, CloudFormation, and Bicep are common. Always plan before you apply. State is the tool's notebook of real resource ids, so protect it.\n\nWhat it solves\nEnvironments become repeatable and reviewable. Click-ops drift is visible. The file is the wish. The tool compares wish versus world.\n\nReal-life example\nA city map you update in git, not chalk on a wall. Preview the haircut before the scissors. The catalog in the locked drawer is state. Do not leave it on a laptop desktop.\n\nUses\nCloud networks, buckets, databases. Interviews: plan vs apply, state, drift.\n\nWatch out\nCommitting terraform.tfstate. Apply without reading a destroy line. Fixing prod in the console and never updating the files." },
    { title: "Environments", body: "The problem before\nDev, staging, and prod are three different recipes. Prod is a unique snowflake. You rebuild because prod needs different compile flags.\n\nWhat this is\nTeams usually have dev, staging, and production, and a build should walk those rooms in order. Use the same image digest in each room. Only config and secrets should change. A human approval can sit in front of prod so promotion stays honest.\n\nWhat it solves\nThe dress rehearsal tested the same box you serve. You can still hide unfinished work with flags. You do not rewrite the script between rooms.\n\nReal-life example\nOne boxed lunch carried through a practice kitchen, then the dining room. The sauce bottle on the table may differ. The box fingerprint must not.\n\nUses\nRelease flow. Interviews: promote the artifact, trunk-based plus flags.\n\nWatch out\nLong-lived prod branches. Building a new image for prod. Changing code between staging and prod and calling it the same release." },
    { title: "Config & secrets", body: "The problem before\nPasswords sit in git and in images. The pipeline uses a human's cloud keys. A leaked .env is still in history after you delete the line.\n\nWhat this is\nConfig is non-secret settings like log level or feature names. Secrets are passwords, tokens, and keys. Config can live in env vars or a store; secrets belong in a vault, not in git. Rotate leaks, and give the pipeline its own least-privilege identity.\n\nWhat it solves\nA leaked robot key should not be a master key. Config can be reviewed. Secrets can be rotated without rebaking the cake.\n\nReal-life example\nA labeled drawer for opening hours versus a safe with an auto-changing lock for the till code. The robot gets its own visitor badge, not your house key.\n\nUses\nApp env, CI OIDC roles, Parameter Store, Secrets Manager. Interviews: what never goes in git.\n\nWatch out\nCommitting .env with real keys. Baking DB passwords into task YAML. Deleting a leaked line and skipping rotation." },
    { title: "Observability", body: "The problem before\nYou only know is it up. Checkout is slow and each service has a private diary. Alerts page on CPU 81 percent. Unique user ids explode the metric cabinet.\n\nWhat this is\nObservability is how you understand a live system from what it emits. Logs are events, metrics are numbers over time, and traces follow one request across services. Alerts should page on user pain, not every CPU blip. Put a request id on the whole story, and let SLIs and SLOs beat noisy CPU rules.\n\nWhat it solves\nYou can ask why checkout is slow. One sticker ties the diary, the speedometer, and the GPS track. Quiet alerts that matter keep on-call sane.\n\nReal-life example\nA car dashboard plus a mechanic's trace, not only a check-engine light. A diary, a speedometer, and a GPS track of one trip. A filing cabinet that needs a new drawer per human explodes.\n\nUses\nProduction services. Interviews: three pillars, golden signals, SLI SLO SLA.\n\nWatch out\nprint error with no request id. Metric labels like user_id. Paging on every warning. Three tools with three different ids." },
    { title: "Reliability", body: "The problem before\nUptime is a hope and nobody owns the incident. Restores are untested. Features ship while the error pocket is empty, and six people restart the same pod.\n\nWhat this is\nReliability is keeping the promise users care about. SLOs name the target; error budgets name the room to fail. Incidents need owners, comms, and a blameless write-up after. Runbooks tell on-call what to check; chaos is optional, practiced restores are not.\n\nWhat it solves\nFeature versus reliability becomes a number, not a shouting match. Mitigate first, root-cause later. A spare parachute you have opened is a plan.\n\nReal-life example\nShop closed versus receipts vanished: RTO and RPO. Spare tires, photocopies, a second warehouse: HA, backup, DR. A fire drill, not arson, if you inject failure.\n\nUses\nSRE interviews. On-call design. Disaster recovery talk.\n\nWatch out\nA green backup tick with never-restore. Paging for graph wrinkles. Postmortems with no tickets. Chaos in prod with no abort." },
    { title: "Security (DevSecOps)", body: "The problem before\nSecurity is a week at the end. npm audit is the only scan. Keys sit in commits. The cluster cannot tell who baked the image.\n\nWhat this is\nDevSecOps means security joins the same delivery loop. Scan dependencies, source, containers, and IaC on every change, and sign artifacts so the cluster knows who built them. Shift left means find issues earlier, not only in CI; you still need runtime defenses. Least privilege for humans and robots, and rotate anything that leaked.\n\nWhat it solves\nFindings arrive while the change is still small. SAST, DAST, and SCA find different bugs. A metal detector at the door of git catches some keys. Runtime still needs a bouncer.\n\nReal-life example\nWash hands during cooking, not only at dessert. Proofreading a recipe, tasting the soup, checking the spice brands. Signing the lunchbox so the dining room knows the kitchen.\n\nUses\nPR scan jobs. Supply chain interviews. Secret scanning and rotation.\n\nWatch out\nA review the day before launch. Only SCA and calling the app secure. git rm a key without rotation. Signing nothing and hoping the cluster trusts latest." },
    { title: "GitOps & platforms", body: "The problem before\nEvery team invents deploy. CI pushes into the cluster with a long-lived kubeconfig. Live kubectl edit is the source of truth. Drift is a rumor.\n\nWhat this is\nGitOps means git holds the desired state, and a controller in the cluster pulls it. Push deploy from CI is the older sibling: the pipeline talks to the cluster. Internal platforms give teams a paved road: starter repos, default CI, default logs. Teams should not each reinvent deploy.\n\nWhat it solves\nDrift is easier to see when git is the source of truth. The paved road is faster than twenty unique runbooks. Reviewers see YAML diffs like app diffs.\n\nReal-life example\nThe floor plan hangs in the city archive. A clerk in the building pulls the latest plan, rather than a courier shoving furniture from a laptop. A paved road beats twenty dirt tracks to the same market.\n\nUses\nArgo CD, Flux, internal developer platforms. Interviews: pull vs push deploy.\n\nWatch out\nkubectl edit prod as the process. A platform so rigid teams shadow-IT around it. GitOps with secrets committed in plain YAML." },
    { title: "Interview habit", body: "The problem before\nYou recite twenty tools with no number and no rollback. Culture and YAML never meet in one story. You panic on trade-offs.\n\nWhat this is\nIn a DevOps interview, tell one story end to end: commit, CI, artifact, deploy, verify, rollback. Add a number, such as two hours down to fifteen minutes, and mention one outage you would prevent next time. Culture plus automation in the same anecdote. Own one slice deeply, and stay calm about trade-offs.\n\nWhat it solves\nThe interviewer can walk your factory. They hear a loop, not a brand list. A rollback sentence proves you thought past the happy path.\n\nReal-life example\nA new shift manager asks how lunch gets to the table. You walk ticket, taste, box fingerprint, dining room, smoke check, and the fire escape. You do not dump the catalog of oven brands.\n\nUses\nEvery DevOps interview closer. Same habit when you teach a teammate.\n\nWatch out\nTool soup. No metric. No rollback. Pretending there is one correct pipeline for every shop." }
  ],
  questions: [
    { id: 1, level: "beginner", q: "What is DevOps?", a: "DevOps is a way of working, not only a job title. The people who build the app and the people who run it share one loop. They automate checks, ship often, watch production, and learn.\n\nTools help, but tools are not the whole idea. Nobody should throw a finished app over a wall and walk away. The loop is build, test, ship, watch, learn.\n\nIn the code: loop is a list of those five words. join prints them with arrows so you can see the path in order.\n\nA common mistake is naming Jenkins or Kubernetes and stopping. Interviewers want the shared loop, not a tool dump.", code: "# tiny picture of the loop (not a terminal lesson)\nloop = [\n    \"build\",\n    \"test\",\n    \"ship\",\n    \"watch\",\n    \"learn\",\n]\nprint(\" -> \".join(loop))" },
    { id: 2, level: "beginner", q: "DevOps vs SRE vs platform?", a: "DevOps is the culture of sharing delivery. SRE uses software and SLOs to run systems, in the Google style. Platform teams build a paved road so many app teams can deploy safely.\n\nThey overlap in real companies. Do not fight over labels. Say what each hat is for, then admit the overlap.\n\nIn the code: roles is a dict with three keys. devops maps to share build + run, sre to SLOs, platform to a paved road. print shows that mapping.\n\nA common mistake is claiming only one of these names is 'real'. Companies mix them.", code: "# three hats, one delivery path\nroles = {\n    \"devops\": \"share build + run\",\n    \"sre\": \"SLOs and error budgets\",\n    \"platform\": \"paved road for many teams\",\n}\nprint(roles)" },
    { id: 3, level: "beginner", q: "What is CI?", a: "CI means Continuous Integration. People merge often and a robot checks every change. The robot runs tests so bugs show up in minutes, not at release week.\n\nThe same checks should work on your laptop and in the pipeline. Waiting a month to combine work is the old painful way. Fast feedback is the point.\n\nIn the code: a GitHub Actions workflow named ci runs on pull_request. The test job uses ubuntu-latest, checks out the code, and would run the project's tests next.\n\nA common mistake is running tests only on your laptop. Then the merge surprises everyone else.", code: "# .github/workflows/ci.yml\nname: ci\non:\n  pull_request:\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      # next step would run the project's tests" },
    { id: 4, level: "beginner", q: "What is CD?", a: "Continuous Delivery means main is always ready to ship. Continuous Deployment means every green main actually goes to production. People mix the letters, so you should define them.\n\nApprovals can still sit in front of prod. The artifact is already built. Delivery packs the suitcase. Deployment sends the taxi.\n\nIn the code: cd is a dict with delivery and deployment sentences. print shows each definition on its own line.\n\nA common mistake is saying 'we do CD' without saying which meaning. Ask which one they mean, then answer.", code: "# delivery vs deployment as data\ncd = {\n    \"delivery\": \"main is always releasable\",\n    \"deployment\": \"green main goes live\",\n}\nprint(cd[\"delivery\"])\nprint(cd[\"deployment\"])" },
    { id: 5, level: "beginner", q: "What is a pipeline?", a: "A pipeline is a series of jobs in order. Typical jobs are checkout, install, test, build, scan, publish, deploy. A failed early job should stop the later dangerous ones.\n\nStages are those checkpoints. Fail fast on unit tests before you ship. The pipeline is the airport security line for your code.\n\nIn the code: stages is that list of gates. enumerate prints 1 checkout, 2 test, and so on. The comment says later stages do not run if test fails.\n\nA common mistake is deploying even when tests are red. Then CI is decoration.", code: "# pipeline as a list of gates\nstages = [\"checkout\", \"test\", \"build\", \"scan\", \"publish\"]\nfor i, name in enumerate(stages, start=1):\n    print(i, name)\n# if test fails, later stages do not run" },
    { id: 6, level: "beginner", q: "What is an artifact?", a: "An artifact is the packaged output of a build: a Docker image, a jar, or a zip of files. You should promote that same box through environments. Rebuilding later can pick up different libraries and surprise you.\n\nTag it with a git sha so you know which source it came from. Immutable means you do not edit the box; you bake a new one. The artifact is what you tested.\n\nIn the code: artifact is a dict with kind docker-image and tag abc123 as the git sha. print says to promote this exact box.\n\nA common mistake is rebuilding for production and calling it the same release. The bytes may differ.", code: "# artifact identity\nartifact = {\n    \"kind\": \"docker-image\",\n    \"tag\": \"abc123\",   # git sha\n}\nprint(\"promote this exact box\", artifact)" },
    { id: 7, level: "beginner", q: "Why promote the same artifact?", a: "What you tested in staging should be what you ship. A new build can pull a new nested library and break the release. Same digest, different config, is the safe story.\n\nConfig may differ: env names and secret refs. The image id should not. Pin the digest, not only a moving tag like latest.\n\nIn the code: staging and prod both use app@sha256:aaa. The equality print is True. The comment notes config may still differ.\n\nA common mistake is tagging latest in staging and latest in prod. Those two latests can be different images hours apart.", code: "staging = {\"image\": \"app@sha256:aaa\"}\nprod = {\"image\": \"app@sha256:aaa\"}   # same\nprint(staging[\"image\"] == prod[\"image\"])\n# config may differ: env names, secret refs" },
    { id: 8, level: "beginner", q: "What is a build vs a release?", a: "A build compiles and packages. A release is the decision to make that build available: tag, notes, deploy. Not every build is a release.\n\nCI makes many packs. Humans or rules choose the shop date. Keep the words separate in interviews.\n\nIn the code: build id 91 is ok. release points at that build_id, to prod, when approved. The prints separate packing from deciding.\n\nA common mistake is calling every green CI run a production release. Shipping is a separate choice.", code: "build = {\"id\": 91, \"ok\": True}\nrelease = {\"build_id\": 91, \"to\": \"prod\", \"when\": \"approved\"}\nprint(\"build packs; release decides\")\nprint(release)" },
    { id: 9, level: "beginner", q: "GitHub Actions in one minute?", a: "GitHub Actions uses YAML files under .github/workflows. A trigger can be a push, a pull request, or a schedule. Jobs run on runners. Steps use actions or scripts.\n\nSecrets live in repo or org settings, not in the YAML. The YAML is the robot's recipe. The robot wakes up when git changes.\n\nIn the code: the workflow named test runs on push to main. The unit job checks out the repo, then setup-node with version 20.\n\nA common mistake is pasting cloud keys into the YAML. Use the secrets store and short-lived roles.", code: "name: test\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\njobs:\n  unit:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4\n        with:\n          node-version: \"20\"" },
    { id: 10, level: "beginner", q: "Jenkins vs GitHub Actions?", a: "Jenkins is often self-hosted, plugin-rich, and very flexible. GitHub Actions sits next to GitHub and is hosted for you. Both can test, build, and deploy.\n\nThe ops cost is the real difference. Pick based on where the code lives and who patches the runners. Same idea, different home.\n\nIn the code: jenkinsfile is a tiny pipeline string. actions is a tiny on: push jobs string. print says same idea, different home, then shows the start of the Jenkins line.\n\nA common mistake is arguing brand loyalty. Interviewers want when you would pick each.", code: "# both are \"pipeline as code\"\njenkinsfile = \"pipeline { stages { ... } }\"\nactions = \"on: push\\njobs: test: ...\"\nprint(\"same idea, different home\")\nprint(jenkinsfile[:18])" },
    { id: 11, level: "intermediate", q: "pipeline as code benefits?", a: "The pipeline can be reviewed like app code. Branches get the same checks. You can read history when something breaks.\n\nA GUI-only pipeline is a secret recipe in someone's account. Store the workflow next to the app. Reviewers see YAML diffs in the same PR.\n\nIn the code: the ci workflow runs on pull_request. The test job checks out the code. The comment says this file's diff is part of code review.\n\nA common mistake is editing production jobs only in a web UI. Then nobody can grep the history.", code: "# the workflow file is reviewed in the same PR\nname: ci\non: [pull_request]\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      # this file's diff is part of code review" },
    { id: 12, level: "intermediate", q: "matrix builds?", a: "A matrix runs the same job across versions, like Node 18 and 20. You catch works on my version only bugs. Keep the matrix small so CI stays fast.\n\nIt is still one workflow file. Failing one cell should fail the PR if that version matters. You are tasting the soup on two stoves, not twenty.\n\nIn the code: strategy.matrix.node is 18 and 20. setup-node uses matrix.node so each cell gets its version. The job still has one name.\n\nA common mistake is a huge matrix that takes an hour. People skip waiting and merge anyway.", code: "name: matrix\non: [push]\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    strategy:\n      matrix:\n        node: [\"18\", \"20\"]\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4\n        with:\n          node-version: ${{ matrix.node }}" },
    { id: 13, level: "intermediate", q: "caching in CI?", a: "A cache reuses npm or pip downloads so jobs finish faster. The cache key must change when the lockfile changes. A stale cache can hide a real install bug.\n\nInvalidate on lockfile. Wrong leftovers make a bad dinner that only fails in CI. Cache is a speed trick, not a substitute for a lockfile.\n\nIn the code: actions/cache stores ~/.npm. The key is npm- plus a hash of package-lock.json. When the lockfile changes, the key changes.\n\nA common mistake is caching node_modules forever with a fixed key. Then CI lies about a clean install.", code: "name: ci\non: [push]\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/cache@v4\n        with:\n          path: ~/.npm\n          key: npm-${{ hashFiles('package-lock.json') }}" },
    { id: 14, level: "intermediate", q: "CI should be hermetic — meaning?", a: "Hermetic means the build does not depend on leftover files or latest luck. Pin versions. Use lockfiles. Start from a clean workspace.\n\nTime of day should not change the result. If it only works on one laptop, it is not hermetic. Pin the language version in the workflow.\n\nIn the code: setup-python pins 3.12. checkout gives a clean copy of the repo. The comment names lockfile plus pinned runtime.\n\nA common mistake is using python: latest or node: latest in CI. The floor can move overnight.", code: "name: hermetic\non: [push]\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-python@v5\n        with:\n          python-version: \"3.12\"\n      # lockfile + pinned runtime = repeatable" },
    { id: 15, level: "intermediate", q: "flaky tests?", a: "A flaky test fails sometimes for no real bug. People rerun until green and stop trusting CI. Fix timing, order, and shared state.\n\nQuarantine only as a short hospital stay, not a home. If the alarm beeps for toast, soon nobody leaves the building. Deterministic tests are the point of CI.\n\nIn the code: add(a, b) returns a + b. The check add(2, 3) === 5 is stable. Same input, same output, no clock.\n\nA common mistake is retrying a red test until it passes and calling that a fix. You trained the team to ignore CI.", code: "// test must not depend on wall-clock luck\nfunction add(a, b) {\n  return a + b;\n}\n// good: same input, same output\nconsole.log(add(2, 3) === 5);" },
    { id: 16, level: "intermediate", q: "test pyramid in CI?", a: "Many fast unit tests, fewer integration tests, few end-to-end tests. Slow e2e can sit on main or a night job. PR CI must stay quick or people skip it.\n\nThe pyramid has a wide base and a small top. The top is expensive and brittle. Put the cheap net at the bottom.\n\nIn the code: pyramid maps unit 100, integration 20, e2e 5. print shows those counts. The comment says PR jobs run unit plus a little integration.\n\nA common mistake is only e2e tests. Then every PR waits on a slow, flaky browser run.", code: "pyramid = {\n    \"unit\": 100,\n    \"integration\": 20,\n    \"e2e\": 5,\n}\nprint(pyramid)\n# PR job runs unit + a little integration" },
    { id: 17, level: "intermediate", q: "required status checks?", a: "Branch protection says do not merge until this job is green. That is the social contract that makes CI real. Without it, red checks become optional decoration.\n\nName the exact job in the protection rule. Humans still review; the robot only proves tests ran. The door should not open until the badge is green.\n\nIn the code: the job id is test and the name is unit-tests. Required checks must match that name. checkout still happens first.\n\nA common mistake is requiring a job name that does not match the YAML. Then protection never sees a green check.", code: "# required check name must match this job id\nname: ci\non: [pull_request]\njobs:\n  test:\n    name: unit-tests\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4" },
    { id: 18, level: "intermediate", q: "self-hosted runners?", a: "Self-hosted runners are your VMs or Kubernetes pods. You get private networks, GPUs, or lower cost at scale. You also own patching and security.\n\nHosted runners are less ops. runs-on selects who executes the job. Your kitchen has your spices and your fire risk.\n\nIn the code: runs-on is [self-hosted, linux]. checkout still pulls the repo. The comment says this box sits in your network.\n\nA common mistake is leaving self-hosted runners unpatched with admin cloud keys. A stolen runner is a stolen cloud.", code: "# runs-on selects who executes the job\nname: ci\non: [push]\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: [self-hosted, linux]\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      # this box sits in your network" },
    { id: 19, level: "intermediate", q: "OIDC in CI?", a: "OIDC lets GitHub prove who it is and assume a short-lived cloud role. You do not store AWS access keys in GitHub secrets if you can avoid it. The trust is scoped to repo and environment.\n\nThe workflow requests a token; the cloud checks the token. This is the preferred modern pattern. A visitor badge that expires beats a spare house key in a drawer.\n\nIn the code: permissions id-token: write is required for OIDC. configure-aws-credentials assumes role arn:aws:iam::123:role/gha.\n\nA common mistake is long-lived AKIA keys in GitHub secrets. If they leak, they work until someone rotates them.", code: "name: deploy\non: [push]\njobs:\n  aws:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    permissions:\n      id-token: write   # needed for OIDC\n    steps:\n      - uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4\n        with:\n          role-to-assume: arn:aws:iam::123:role/gha" },
    { id: 20, level: "intermediate", q: "What should never be in git for DevOps?", a: "Cloud keys, kubeconfigs, .env files, and tfvars with passwords must not be committed. State files can also hold secrets. Use a vault or the host's secret store.\n\n.gitignore helps but humans still slip. Rotate anything that leaked. Deleting the line later does not un-leak the git history.\n\nIn the code: never lists .env, *.pem, terraform.tfstate, and kubeconfig. The loop prints keep out of git for each name.\n\nA common mistake is committing a .pem 'just for today'. Today becomes forever in git history.", code: "# .gitignore idea as a list\nnever = [\".env\", \"*.pem\", \"terraform.tfstate\", \"kubeconfig\"]\nfor name in never:\n    print(\"keep out of git:\", name)" },
    { id: 21, level: "beginner", q: "What is Infrastructure as Code?", a: "You describe servers, networks, and buckets in files. Then a tool makes reality match the files. Environments become repeatable and reviewable.\n\nClick-ops drift is the enemy. Terraform HCL is one popular language for that blueprint. The file is the wish; the tool compares wish vs world.\n\nIn the code: an aws_s3_bucket resource named photos wants bucket my-app-photos. That is the declared wish, not a click in the console.\n\nA common mistake is creating prod buckets by hand and then wondering why Terraform wants to destroy them.", code: "# teaching Terraform: describe, do not \"run apply\" here\nresource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"photos\" {\n  bucket = \"my-app-photos\"\n}\n# this file is the wish; the tool compares wish vs world" },
    { id: 22, level: "intermediate", q: "Terraform state?", a: "State is Terraform's notebook of real resource ids it manages. Teams store it remotely with a lock so two people do not write at once. Do not edit it casually.\n\nIt can contain secrets, so do not commit it. If the catalog is wrong, the next change is dangerous. Protect it like production data.\n\nIn the code: the backend is S3 with bucket tf-state-prod, key app/terraform.tfstate, region ap-south-1. That is remote state, not a local file in git.\n\nA common mistake is committing terraform.tfstate. It can hold secrets and it goes stale on every laptop.", code: "# remote state backend (teaching)\nterraform {\n  backend \"s3\" {\n    bucket = \"tf-state-prod\"\n    key    = \"app/terraform.tfstate\"\n    region = \"ap-south-1\"\n  }\n}" },
    { id: 23, level: "intermediate", q: "terraform plan vs apply?", a: "plan shows the diff: add, change, destroy. apply makes that diff real. In CI, people often plan on a PR and apply on main.\n\nAlways read the plan. Preview the haircut before the scissors. This file teaches the two ideas as HCL, not a command list.\n\nIn the code: aws_s3_bucket logs wants my-app-logs. The comment says read the plan: create bucket, no destroy.\n\nA common mistake is apply without reading a destroy line. That is how accidental data loss happens.", code: "# plan = show this diff; apply = do it\nresource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"logs\" {\n  bucket = \"my-app-logs\"\n}\n# read the plan: create bucket, no destroy" },
    { id: 24, level: "intermediate", q: "drift?", a: "Drift means reality no longer matches the files. Someone clicked in the console. The next plan may want to destroy a surprise.\n\nPolicy: change via IaC, or import the click into the files. Detect drift; do not ignore it. A map that misses a new one-way street causes crashes.\n\nIn the code: public access block is all true for my-app-photos. If the console made the bucket public, that file and the world disagree.\n\nA common mistake is fixing prod in the console and never updating the files. The next apply fights you.", code: "# file says private; console made it public -> drift\nresource \"aws_s3_bucket_public_access_block\" \"photos\" {\n  bucket                  = \"my-app-photos\"\n  block_public_acls       = true\n  block_public_policy     = true\n  ignore_public_acls      = true\n  restrict_public_buckets = true\n}" },
    { id: 25, level: "intermediate", q: "immutable infrastructure?", a: "You bake a new image or container and roll it out. You do not SSH in to just fix prod. New versions replace old ones.\n\nRollback is another known image. Cattle, not pets. Swap a whole light bulb; do not tape a broken one.\n\nIn the code: service maps image to app:sha-bbb and old to app:sha-aaa. print says replace the old with the new image id.\n\nA common mistake is SSHing into prod to patch a file. The next scale-out boots the old image and the bug returns.", code: "# new version = new image id, not an edited VM\nservice = {\n    \"image\": \"app:sha-bbb\",\n    \"old\": \"app:sha-aaa\",\n}\nprint(\"replace\", service[\"old\"], \"with\", service[\"image\"])" },
    { id: 26, level: "beginner", q: "pets vs cattle?", a: "Pets have names and you nurse them when they are sick. Cattle have numbers and you replace them. Cloud VMs and pods should be cattle.\n\nIf egg 47 is bad, you take another egg. That is how autoscaling works in your head. Named snowflake boxes do not scale.\n\nIn the code: pet has name web01 and fix ssh and patch. cattle has id 47 and fix replace instance. print shows the cattle dict.\n\nA common mistake is treating one prod VM as irreplaceable. Then you cannot deploy or fail over cleanly.", code: "pet = {\"name\": \"web01\", \"fix\": \"ssh and patch\"}\ncattle = {\"id\": 47, \"fix\": \"replace instance\"}\nprint(cattle)\n# teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 27, level: "intermediate", q: "configuration management vs IaC?", a: "Configuration management configures machines that already exist: packages and files. IaC provisions the cloud objects themselves: VPC, bucket, database. There is overlap.\n\nMany teams use Terraform for cloud and a little Ansible for leftovers. Buying the house versus arranging the furniture. Both can be files in git.\n\nIn the code: aws_instance web sets ami and t3.micro. That creates the box. Config management would then set files inside the OS.\n\nA common mistake is using Ansible to create the whole cloud by hand-shaped tasks. Provisioning tools fit that job better.", code: "# IaC creates the box\nresource \"aws_instance\" \"web\" {\n  ami           = \"ami-123\"\n  instance_type = \"t3.micro\"\n}\n# config mgmt would then set files inside the OS" },
    { id: 28, level: "intermediate", q: "Ansible in one minute?", a: "Ansible pushes YAML playbooks, often over SSH, with no agent on the target. Modules try to be idempotent: run twice, same result. It is good for VMs and network devices.\n\nIt is a weak only-orchestrator for containers. The playbook is a checklist a robot reads to each server. Learn the shape even if you live in Kubernetes.\n\nIn the code: a play named ensure app user targets hosts web. The user module wants name app and state present.\n\nA common mistake is writing shell: commands for everything. Then a second run is not safe or idempotent.", code: "# playbook shape\n- name: ensure app user\n  hosts: web\n  tasks:\n    - name: create user\n      ansible.builtin.user:\n        name: app\n        state: present" },
    { id: 29, level: "intermediate", q: "environment promotion?", a: "dev, then staging, then prod. The image digest stays the same. Config and secrets change.\n\nA human approval can sit on prod. Do not rewrite the script between rooms. Promote the artifact.\n\nIn the code: digest is sha256:abc. envs lists dev, staging, and prod all using that digest. print shows the prod dict.\n\nA common mistake is building a new image for prod because 'prod needs different compile flags'. Then staging tested something else.", code: "digest = \"sha256:abc\"\nenvs = [\n    {\"name\": \"dev\", \"image\": digest},\n    {\"name\": \"staging\", \"image\": digest},\n    {\"name\": \"prod\", \"image\": digest},\n]\nprint(envs[-1])" },
    { id: 30, level: "intermediate", q: "feature flags vs env branches?", a: "Env branches diverge and merges become painful. Prefer trunk-based development plus flags. Flags hide unfinished work on the same main.\n\nHotfix branches can still exist for emergencies. Flags need cleanup later. One road with traffic lights beats three secret roads that never meet.\n\nIn the code: flags.newCheckout is false. price(100) returns 100 because the new path is off. The same binary can turn the path on later.\n\nA common mistake is a long-lived prod branch that only merges once a quarter. CI then tests a world that is not main.", code: "// same binary, flag decides the path\nconst flags = { newCheckout: false };\nfunction price(total) {\n  if (flags.newCheckout) return total * 0.9;\n  return total;\n}\nconsole.log(price(100));" },
    { id: 31, level: "intermediate", q: "trunk-based development?", a: "People merge to main often. Incomplete work hides behind flags. That enables real CI because main is always the integration point.\n\nLong-lived release branches are the opposite pattern. Keep branches measured in hours or days. One main river, not five canals that never rejoin.\n\nIn the code: work is a dict with branch feat-login, hours_open 8, merged_to main. print shows a short-lived branch story.\n\nA common mistake is feature branches that live for months. Merge pain and 'it worked on my branch' follow.", code: "# branch lifetime picture\nwork = {\"branch\": \"feat-login\", \"hours_open\": 8, \"merged_to\": \"main\"}\nprint(work)\n# long-lived: months_open would fight CI" },
    { id: 32, level: "beginner", q: "blue-green deploy?", a: "Blue is live. Green is the new version idle, or the reverse names. When green is healthy, you switch traffic.\n\nRollback is switching back. You pay for two runtimes during the flip. Health checks matter before the switch.\n\nIn the code: live is blue. stacks maps blue to app:v1 and green to app:v2. print says users see app:v1. After health ok you would set live to green.\n\nA common mistake is switching traffic before health checks pass. Then both colors can look down.", code: "# traffic pointer\nlive = \"blue\"\nstacks = {\"blue\": \"app:v1\", \"green\": \"app:v2\"}\nprint(\"users see\", stacks[live])\n# after health ok: live = \"green\"" },
    { id: 33, level: "beginner", q: "rolling deploy?", a: "A rolling deploy updates a few boxes, then a few more. It is cheaper than a full extra environment. APIs and database changes must still work with mixed versions.\n\nNeed backward-compatible steps. Health checks pull sick cars out. You change train cars one at a time while the train moves.\n\nIn the code: versions starts as four v1. Then versions[0] becomes v2. print shows mixed versions during the roll.\n\nA common mistake is a breaking API change mid-roll. Old boxes and new boxes then cannot talk to the same clients.", code: "# 4 instances, replace 1 at a time\nversions = [\"v1\", \"v1\", \"v1\", \"v1\"]\nversions[0] = \"v2\"\nprint(\"mixed versions during roll\", versions)" },
    { id: 34, level: "intermediate", q: "canary deploy?", a: "A canary sends a little traffic to the new version. You watch errors and latency, then grow the percent. You need good metrics and a way to split traffic.\n\nIf the sip is bad, you stop. Observability is the canary's oxygen. Start small, then raise the share.\n\nIn the code: traffic is v1 95 percent and v2 5 percent. print shows the canary percent 5. The comment says set v2 back to 0 if error rate is high.\n\nA common mistake is a canary with no dashboards. Then 5 percent failing is invisible.", code: "traffic = {\"v1\": 95, \"v2\": 5}\nprint(\"canary percent\", traffic[\"v2\"])\n# if error_rate(v2) is high, set v2 back to 0\n# teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 35, level: "intermediate", q: "feature flag vs canary?", a: "A canary is a deploy strategy: some users hit a new binary. A flag toggles behavior even on the same binary. You can use both: canary the binary, flag the risky feature.\n\nFlags are for product behavior. Canaries are for release risk. A new car model versus a button that hides the turbo.\n\nIn the code: version is v2 and flags.turbo is still false. print shows both knobs together.\n\nA common mistake is treating a flag as a canary. Flags do not prove the new binary is healthy.", code: "const version = \"v2\";          // canary binary\nconst flags = { turbo: false }; // still off\nconsole.log({ version, flags });\n// teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 36, level: "intermediate", q: "rollback plan?", a: "You redeploy the previous artifact, revert git, or turn a flag off. The database schema must still work with the old app. Practice the plan.\n\nA plan that only exists in a slide is not a plan. Expand/contract keeps rollback possible. Walk the fire escape before you need it.\n\nIn the code: releases is v3 then v4. print says rollback to v3. The comment notes turning a flag off can be even faster.\n\nA common mistake is a forward-only migration that the old app cannot read. Then rollback is blocked.", code: "releases = [\"v3\", \"v4\"]   # v4 is bad\nprint(\"rollback to\", releases[0])\n# flags: riskyFeature = false is even faster\n# teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 37, level: "intermediate", q: "database migrations in CD?", a: "Run a migration once, as a gated step, in a backward-compatible way. Do not let ten app copies all auto migrate at once without a lock. Order: safe schema, then code, then cleanup.\n\nCI can apply the migration job before the new app. Locks prevent double-run races. Change the highway while cars still drive: add a lane first.\n\nIn the code: migration says add users.nickname nullable and old_app_ok True. print shows that expand step.\n\nA common mistake is DROP COLUMN in the same release as the new code. Old boxes still running then crash.", code: "# expand step: add column, old code ignores it\nmigration = {\n    \"step\": \"add users.nickname nullable\",\n    \"old_app_ok\": True,\n}\nprint(migration)" },
    { id: 38, level: "advanced", q: "expand/contract in DevOps terms?", a: "Expand: add new columns or tables. Deploy code that can read both old and new. Migrate data. Switch. Contract: remove the old.\n\nThat decouples DB and app so rollback still works. Never drop a column the old app still needs. Build a new bridge beside the old one, then tear the old one.\n\nIn the code: phases is add column, dual write, switch reads, drop old. join prints them with arrows.\n\nA common mistake is contract first. Then you cannot roll back the app.", code: "phases = [\"add column\", \"dual write\", \"switch reads\", \"drop old\"]\nprint(\" -> \".join(phases))\n# teaching snippet: read the comments\n# this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 39, level: "intermediate", q: "health checks in deploy?", a: "A health check is a cheap question: are you ready for traffic? The balancer only sends users to instances that pass. A deploy that skips health is a hope.\n\nIn Kubernetes, liveness and readiness are different questions. Wrong checks restart healthy apps. The bouncer only lets healthy waiters onto the floor.\n\nIn the code: health returns 200 and { ok: true }. The comment says do not hit the database if a blink should not drop you.\n\nA common mistake is a health URL that queries the database. A short DB blip then marks every app instance dead.", code: "// cheap readiness answer\nfunction health(req, res) {\n  res.status(200).json({ ok: true });\n}\n// do not hit the database if a blink should not drop you" },
    { id: 40, level: "intermediate", q: "smoke tests?", a: "Smoke tests are a few real checks: homepage 200, login, one critical API. They catch the box is up but the app is wrong. Do them before you walk away.\n\nKeep them tiny and stable. Alert if they fail. Checking that the stove lights is not a full banquet.\n\nIn the code: smoke fetches base + \"/\" and returns whether status is 200. The log line says a tiny real request, not a full suite.\n\nA common mistake is no post-deploy check. Then you learn from users that checkout is 500.", code: "// post-deploy smoke (teaching)\nasync function smoke(base) {\n  const home = await fetch(base + \"/\");\n  return home.status === 200;\n}\nconsole.log(\"smoke is a tiny real request, not a full suite\");" },
    { id: 41, level: "beginner", q: "What is observability?", a: "Observability is asking why it behaves this way from its outputs. Logs, metrics, traces, and profiles are those outputs. Monitoring is often is it up. Observability is why is checkout slow.\n\nYou design what you emit. A dashboard without a request id is a guess. Build the car dashboard and the mechanic traces, not only a check-engine light.\n\nIn the code: log is an object with level info, msg checkout started, and request_id abc-123. JSON.stringify makes it queryable.\n\nA common mistake is print('error') with no request id. Then you cannot join that line to the user's report.", code: "// one event you can query later\nconst log = {\n  level: \"info\",\n  msg: \"checkout started\",\n  request_id: \"abc-123\",\n};\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify(log));" },
    { id: 42, level: "intermediate", q: "three pillars?", a: "Logs are events. Metrics are numbers over time. Traces follow one request across services. You want a request id on all three.\n\nOne pillar is not enough for microservices. Correlate them. A diary, a speedometer, and a GPS track of one trip.\n\nIn the code: request_id is abc-123 on the log, the metric pay_ok, and the trace spans api and db. print shows the metric and the span list.\n\nA common mistake is three tools with three different ids. Then you cannot stitch one user journey.", code: "const request_id = \"abc-123\";\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify({ request_id, msg: \"paid\" }));\nconst metric = { name: \"pay_ok\", value: 1, request_id };\nconst trace = { request_id, spans: [\"api\", \"db\"] };\nconsole.log(metric, trace.spans);" },
    { id: 43, level: "intermediate", q: "SLI, SLO, SLA?", a: "SLI is the measure, like success rate. SLO is the target, like 99.9%. SLA is the customer contract and penalty.\n\nSRE uses SLOs even when there is no legal SLA. Do not promise an SLA you cannot measure. Scale, goal weight, gym contract.\n\nIn the code: sli is 0.9991, slo is 0.999, sla is 0.99. print says the sli meets the slo because 0.9991 >= 0.999.\n\nA common mistake is calling a hope an SLA. If you cannot measure it, you cannot contract it.", code: "sli = 0.9991          # measured\nslo = 0.999           # target\nsla = 0.99            # contract\nprint(\"sli\", sli, \"meets slo\", sli >= slo)" },
    { id: 44, level: "intermediate", q: "error budget?", a: "The error budget is 1 minus the SLO. It is the room to fail. If you burn it, you freeze features and work on reliability.\n\nIt makes the feature versus reliability fight explicit. When the pocket is empty, no more candy. This is an SRE teaching idea.\n\nIn the code: slo is 0.999 so budget is 0.001. burned is 0.002. print shows over is True because burned > budget.\n\nA common mistake is shipping features while the budget is already gone. Then you are choosing outages on purpose.", code: "slo = 0.999\nbudget = 1 - slo\nburned = 0.002\nprint(\"budget\", budget, \"burned\", burned, \"over\", burned > budget)" },
    { id: 45, level: "intermediate", q: "alert fatigue?", a: "If everything pages, people ignore pages. Alert on user-facing pain, not every CPU blip. Every page needs a severity and a runbook.\n\nQuiet alerts that matter. Tune or delete noisy ones. A car alarm that goes off for leaves trains neighbors to ignore it.\n\nIn the code: alerts lists checkout_error_rate as page True and cpu_81_percent as page False. The list comprehension prints only the paging names.\n\nA common mistake is paging on CPU 80%. That wakes people for work that is still healthy.", code: "alerts = [\n    {\"on\": \"checkout_error_rate\", \"page\": True},\n    {\"on\": \"cpu_81_percent\", \"page\": False},\n]\nprint([a[\"on\"] for a in alerts if a[\"page\"]])" },
    { id: 46, level: "intermediate", q: "four golden signals?", a: "Latency, traffic, errors, saturation. They cover is it slow, busy, failing, or full. A solid starting board for a service.\n\nYou can add more later. Name all four in an interview. Wait time, number of patients, wrong treatments, beds full.\n\nIn the code: golden maps latency 0.2s, traffic 120 rps, errors 0.2%, saturation cpu 55%. print shows the four signals.\n\nA common mistake is only graphing CPU. Then a slow downstream dependency never shows up.", code: "golden = {\n    \"latency\": \"0.2s\",\n    \"traffic\": \"120 rps\",\n    \"errors\": \"0.2%\",\n    \"saturation\": \"cpu 55%\",\n}\nprint(golden)" },
    { id: 47, level: "intermediate", q: "RED vs USE?", a: "RED is for services: rate, errors, duration. USE is for resources: utilization, saturation, errors. They complement each other.\n\nUse RED on APIs. Use USE on disks and CPUs. You do not pick only one forever. Watch the shop and watch the oven.\n\nIn the code: red is rate, errors, duration. use is utilization, saturation, errors. print labels them service and resource.\n\nA common mistake is mixing the recipes. Duration is not the same as utilization.", code: "red = [\"rate\", \"errors\", \"duration\"]\nuse = [\"utilization\", \"saturation\", \"errors\"]\nprint(\"service\", red)\nprint(\"resource\", use)" },
    { id: 48, level: "intermediate", q: "structured logging?", a: "Structured logs are fields: level, msg, request_id. Machines can filter them. Humans can still read them.\n\nNever log passwords. One request_id ties the story. Use a logger helper. A labeled form beats a diary poem.\n\nIn the code: log(level, msg, extra) JSON.stringifies those fields plus extra. The call logs payment failed with request_id abc and code card.\n\nA common mistake is logging the full credit card or password 'for debugging'. That is an incident of its own.", code: "function log(level, msg, extra) {\n  console.log(JSON.stringify({ level, msg, ...extra }));\n}\nlog(\"error\", \"payment failed\", { request_id: \"abc\", code: \"card\" });" },
    { id: 49, level: "intermediate", q: "cardinality explosion?", a: "Cardinality is how many unique label combos exist. Unbounded labels like user_id can kill Prometheus memory. Keep metric labels low-cardinality.\n\nPut unique ids in logs and traces. Metrics are for aggregates. A filing cabinet that needs a new drawer per human explodes.\n\nIn the code: the JSON log has msg login and user_id u_99. The comments say user_id is a log field, not a metric label.\n\nA common mistake is a metric named requests{user_id=...}. The time series count becomes millions.", code: "// bad metric label: user_id\n// good log field: user_id\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify({\n  msg: \"login\",\n  user_id: \"u_99\",          // log/trace, not a metric label\n}));" },
    { id: 50, level: "intermediate", q: "distributed tracing?", a: "You pass a trace id through HTTP or gRPC headers. Then you see which hop was slow. OpenTelemetry is the usual name to mention.\n\nStart at the edge API. Without the sticker, each warehouse has a private story. A tracking sticker on a parcel as it visits warehouses.\n\nIn the code: TRACE is the header name traceparent. passTrace copies the incoming header or makes 00-abc-span-01. print shows that object.\n\nA common mistake is generating a new random id in every service. Then you cannot follow one request.", code: "const TRACE = \"traceparent\";\nfunction passTrace(headers) {\n  return { [TRACE]: headers[TRACE] || \"00-abc-span-01\" };\n}\nconsole.log(passTrace({}));" },
    { id: 51, level: "intermediate", q: "OpenTelemetry?", a: "OpenTelemetry is a vendor-neutral way to emit traces, metrics, and logs. You instrument once and export to Jaeger, Tempo, X-Ray, and others. It reduces rewriting telemetry for a new vendor.\n\nA span is a timed step. One plug, many monitors. Mention it as the current default.\n\nIn the code: span has name db.query, trace_id abc, and ms 42. JSON.stringify is the teaching shape of one span.\n\nA common mistake is tying all code to one vendor's SDK. Then changing backends means rewriting the app.", code: "// teaching: a span is a timed step\nconst span = {\n  name: \"db.query\",\n  trace_id: \"abc\",\n  ms: 42,\n};\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify(span));" },
    { id: 52, level: "beginner", q: "What is an incident?", a: "An incident is an unplanned drop in quality or an outage. Someone owns it, talks to users, mitigates, then reviews. It is not a blame contest in the moment.\n\nMitigate first, root-cause later. Write down the timeline while it is fresh. Roles, not panic.\n\nIn the code: incident has owner commander, state mitigating, impact checkout 5xx. print shows that snapshot.\n\nA common mistake is debugging the root cause while users are still down. Stop the bleeding first.", code: "incident = {\n    \"owner\": \"commander\",\n    \"state\": \"mitigating\",\n    \"impact\": \"checkout 5xx\",\n}\nprint(incident)" },
    { id: 53, level: "intermediate", q: "incident roles?", a: "An incident commander coordinates. Someone handles comms. Someone handles ops. Do not have six people restarting the same pod.\n\nSeparate fix from status page. Practice the roles. One director, not six directors.\n\nIn the code: roles maps commander to decide next step, comms to status page, ops to mitigate. print shows the three jobs.\n\nA common mistake is everyone jumping into kubectl. Then you get two rollbacks and no status update.", code: "roles = {\n    \"commander\": \"decide next step\",\n    \"comms\": \"status page\",\n    \"ops\": \"mitigate\",\n}\nprint(roles)" },
    { id: 54, level: "intermediate", q: "blameless postmortem?", a: "You write timeline, impact, contributing factors, and actions. You blame missing tests and missing alerts, not a person. If you shame people, they hide mistakes.\n\nActions should be real tickets. Follow up or it was theater. A flight recorder, not a courtroom.\n\nIn the code: postmortem maps what to bad deploy, why_system to no smoke test, action to add smoke job. print shows that write-up.\n\nA common mistake is a postmortem with no tickets. Then the same outage repeats.", code: "postmortem = {\n    \"what\": \"bad deploy\",\n    \"why_system\": \"no smoke test\",\n    \"action\": \"add smoke job\",\n}\nprint(postmortem)" },
    { id: 55, level: "intermediate", q: "runbook?", a: "A runbook is a short if this alert, check these things guide. It sits next to the alert. If it is a novel, automate more of it.\n\nNew on-call people need this. Keep it next to the code or the alert. Pull, aim, squeeze — not a memoir.\n\nIn the code: runbook is open dashboard, check error rate, rollback if last deploy < 30m. join prints those steps.\n\nA common mistake is an alert with no runbook. Then 3am is guesswork.", code: "runbook = [\n    \"open dashboard\",\n    \"check error rate\",\n    \"rollback if last deploy < 30m\",\n]\nprint(\"\\n\".join(runbook))" },
    { id: 56, level: "intermediate", q: "on-call hygiene?", a: "Rotate. Handoff. Page only when a human must act. Pay for the load. Burnout is an ops risk, not only a feeling.\n\nA 3am page for a graph wrinkle is malpractice. Tune alerts as part of the job. Night shifts exist for a reason; they should be rare and real.\n\nIn the code: page needs_human True and user_impact True. print pages only when both are true.\n\nA common mistake is paging on every warning. People mute the phone and miss the real fire.", code: "page = {\"needs_human\": True, \"user_impact\": True}\nprint(\"page\", page[\"needs_human\"] and page[\"user_impact\"])\n# teaching snippet: read the comments\n# this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 57, level: "beginner", q: "RTO and RPO?", a: "RTO is how long you can be down. RPO is how much data you can lose. They drive how often you back up and how you design HA.\n\nA daily backup cannot meet a 5-minute RPO. Say both letters in the interview. Shop closed versus receipts vanished.\n\nIn the code: rto_minutes is 60 and rpo_minutes is 5. print says restore within 60, lose at most 5.\n\nA common mistake is quoting RTO and RPO as the same number. They measure different pains.", code: "rto_minutes = 60\nrpo_minutes = 5\nprint(\"restore within\", rto_minutes, \"lose at most\", rpo_minutes)\n# teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 58, level: "intermediate", q: "backup vs HA vs DR?", a: "HA survives a single failure with little downtime. Backup restores data after loss. DR recovers from a whole site or region loss.\n\nYou mix them to match RTO and RPO. HA is not a backup by itself. Spare tires, photocopies, a second warehouse.\n\nIn the code: plan maps ha to two AZs, backup to snapshots, dr to second region. print shows all three.\n\nA common mistake is Multi-AZ RDS with no backups. HA does not undo a bad DELETE.", code: "plan = {\n    \"ha\": \"two AZs\",\n    \"backup\": \"snapshots\",\n    \"dr\": \"second region\",\n}\nprint(plan)" },
    { id: 59, level: "intermediate", q: "test your restores?", a: "You must actually restore into a safe place on a schedule. Encryption keys and IAM must work during the drill too. A green backup job tick is not a restore.\n\nWrite the drill as a runbook. Fix what fails in the drill. A spare parachute you have never opened is hope.\n\nIn the code: drill has backup_ok True and restore_ok False. print says hope because restore was not proven.\n\nA common mistake is never testing restore. The first restore is then during the real outage.", code: "drill = {\n    \"backup_ok\": True,\n    \"restore_ok\": False,  # the real test\n}\nprint(\"hope\" if not drill[\"restore_ok\"] else \"ready\")" },
    { id: 60, level: "intermediate", q: "chaos engineering?", a: "You inject failure on purpose: kill a pod, add latency. You learn weaknesses in a controlled way. Start in staging and with game days.\n\nIt is not random sabotage of prod. Have abort criteria. A fire drill, not arson.\n\nIn the code: chaos maps kill to one pod and expect to service stays up. print shows the hypothesis.\n\nA common mistake is chaos in prod with no abort button. Then you created an incident on purpose with no plan.", code: "# experiment hypothesis\nchaos = {\n    \"kill\": \"one pod\",\n    \"expect\": \"service stays up\",\n}\nprint(chaos)" },
    { id: 61, level: "beginner", q: "What is DevSecOps?", a: "DevSecOps means scans and checks in the same delivery loop. Dependency CVEs, SAST, container scans, secret scans, IaC policy. Security is not a week at the end.\n\nYou still need runtime defenses. Shift left means earlier, not only in CI. Wash hands during cooking, not only at dessert.\n\nIn the code: a secure workflow runs on pull_request. The scan job checks out the code. The comment says secret scan and dependency scan belong here.\n\nA common mistake is a security review the day before launch. Then every finding is a fire drill.", code: "name: secure\non: [pull_request]\njobs:\n  scan:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      # secret scan + dependency scan belong here" },
    { id: 62, level: "intermediate", q: "SAST vs DAST vs SCA?", a: "SAST reads code without running it. DAST probes a running app. SCA looks at third-party libraries. They find different bugs.\n\nUse more than one. npm audit is an SCA-style idea. Proofreading a recipe, tasting the soup, checking the spice brands.\n\nIn the code: kinds maps sast to static code, dast to running app, sca to libraries. print shows the three.\n\nA common mistake is running only npm audit and calling the app secure. Your own SQL injection would not show up there.", code: "kinds = {\n    \"sast\": \"static code\",\n    \"dast\": \"running app\",\n    \"sca\": \"libraries\",\n}\nprint(kinds)" },
    { id: 63, level: "intermediate", q: "secret scanning?", a: "Tools look for tokens in commits and block or alert. If something leaked, rotate it; deleting the commit is not enough. Humans still paste keys in tickets.\n\nEducate and rotate. Pre-commit hooks help a bit. A metal detector at the door of git.\n\nIn the code: leak is a fake AKIA-like string. print says the scanner would flag it, then rotate, do not only delete the line.\n\nA common mistake is git rm the key and thinking the history is clean. Assume it is burned; rotate.", code: "# pattern of a leak (fake)\nleak = \"AKIA\" + \"EXAMPLE\"\nprint(\"scanner would flag cloud-like keys\")\nprint(\"then rotate, do not only delete the line\")" },
    { id: 64, level: "intermediate", q: "SBOM in the pipeline?", a: "An SBOM lists what is inside the artifact. You generate it at build, store it, and rescan when new CVEs appear. When a spice is recalled, you know which jars to pull.\n\nThis is increasingly expected. Keep it next to the image. An ingredient label on packaged food.\n\nIn the code: sbom maps image to app:abc and packages to openssl and curl. print shows that list.\n\nA common mistake is not knowing what is in the image. Then a CVE announcement is a scavenger hunt.", code: "sbom = {\n    \"image\": \"app:abc\",\n    \"packages\": [\"openssl\", \"curl\"],\n}\nprint(sbom)" },
    { id: 65, level: "intermediate", q: "signed artifacts / provenance?", a: "You sign the image and record the commit that produced it. Clusters can admit only signed images. That is supply-chain defense.\n\nSLSA and cosign are names you may hear. Unsigned latest from the internet is a trust problem. A wax seal on a letter.\n\nIn the code: provenance maps image to app@sha256:aaa, commit abc123, signed True. print shows that record.\n\nA common mistake is pulling latest from a public registry in prod with no signature check.", code: "provenance = {\n    \"image\": \"app@sha256:aaa\",\n    \"commit\": \"abc123\",\n    \"signed\": True,\n}\nprint(provenance)" },
    { id: 66, level: "intermediate", q: "policy as code?", a: "OPA, Sentinel, Checkov: a PR fails if a bucket is public. Same idea as unit tests, for cloud files. Humans forget; the policy does not.\n\nKeep policies in git. Start with a few high-value rules. A spelling rule that blocks the essay.\n\nIn the code: bucket.public is False. If it were True, SystemExit policy fail would run. print says policy ok.\n\nA common mistake is a 200-rule pack on day one. Teams then bypass the gate.", code: "# policy idea: public buckets denied\nbucket = {\"public\": False}\nif bucket[\"public\"]:\n    raise SystemExit(\"policy fail\")\nprint(\"policy ok\")" },
    { id: 67, level: "beginner", q: "least privilege for pipelines?", a: "The pipeline role should only do what deploy needs. Split plan and apply if you can. OIDC scoped to repo and environment.\n\nA leaked CI token with admin is a bad day. Review the role yearly. A spare key that only opens the garage.\n\nIn the code: the policy allows only ecr:PutImage on the app repository ARN. That is a tight deploy permission.\n\nA common mistake is AdministratorAccess on the GitHub role. One leaked token then owns the account.", code: "{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [{\n    \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n    \"Action\": [\"ecr:PutImage\"],\n    \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:ecr:ap-south-1:123:repository/app\"\n  }]\n}" },
    { id: 68, level: "intermediate", q: "environments in GitHub Actions?", a: "Named environments can have their own secrets and required reviewers. Useful for prod approvals without a separate Jenkins. The workflow names the environment.\n\nReviewers get a GitHub prompt. Dev can stay automatic. A locked cabinet labeled prod.\n\nIn the code: the prod job has environment: production. It still checks out the code. GitHub can require a reviewer on that environment.\n\nA common mistake is storing prod secrets in the same bag as dev. Then every PR job can read prod keys.", code: "name: deploy\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\njobs:\n  prod:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    environment: production\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      # GitHub can require a reviewer on this environment" },
    { id: 69, level: "intermediate", q: "GitOps vs push deploy from CI?", a: "CI push: the pipeline talks to the cluster. GitOps: CI only updates git; a controller in the cluster pulls. GitOps has clearer drift and audit.\n\nBoth are valid. Git becomes the desired state. Mailing a package versus a shop that restocks from a list.\n\nIn the code: app.image is repo/app:sha-abc. The comment says the cluster controller makes live match this file.\n\nA common mistake is kubectl apply from a laptop and also GitOps. Then two sources of truth fight.", code: "# gitops desired state\napp:\n  image: repo/app:sha-abc\n# cluster controller makes live match this file" },
    { id: 70, level: "intermediate", q: "Argo CD / Flux?", a: "Argo CD and Flux watch git and sync the cluster. You see sync status. They do not replace CI tests.\n\nThey replace ad-hoc kubectl from laptops. Hooks can still run jobs like migrations, carefully. Learn one well. Autopilot that follows the map in git.\n\nIn the code: sync source is git, path is deploy/prod, prune true. The comment says live should equal that path.\n\nA common mistake is treating GitOps as a test suite. Broken YAML still ships if CI never ran tests.", code: "# flux/argo idea\nsync:\n  source: git\n  path: deploy/prod\n  prune: true\n# live cluster should equal this path" },
    { id: 71, level: "intermediate", q: "Helm in CD?", a: "Helm packages Kubernetes YAML with values per environment. Pin chart versions. Avoid latest.\n\nKnow how a release rolls back. The mix version is pinned. Values files are the teaching code. A cake mix with a flavor card per country.\n\nIn the code: values-prod sets image repository, tag sha-abc, replicaCount 3. That is env-specific config on a pinned chart.\n\nA common mistake is chart version latest. The next install can change templates under you.", code: "# values-prod.yaml\nimage:\n  repository: repo/app\n  tag: sha-abc\nreplicaCount: 3" },
    { id: 72, level: "beginner", q: "What is a container registry in the flow?", a: "CI pushes. Runtime pulls. Tag with git sha. Scan on push. Retention policies stop infinite storage.\n\nDigest is the true name. The cook grabs a labeled jar, not whatever is on the counter. A library of sealed jars.\n\nIn the code: image maps registry to ghcr.io/org/app and tag to abc123. print shows that identity.\n\nA common mistake is deploying :latest. You cannot say which git commit is running.", code: "image = {\n    \"registry\": \"ghcr.io/org/app\",\n    \"tag\": \"abc123\",\n}\nprint(image)" },
    { id: 73, level: "intermediate", q: "artifact repository?", a: "Nexus, Artifactory, and GitHub Packages store libraries and images. They can cache public packages for speed and reliability. If the public registry blinks, you still have a copy.\n\nCI pulls from here. Access control matters. A warehouse between the internet and your build.\n\nIn the code: repo maps npm to @org/ui@1.2.3 and docker to org/app@sha256:aaa. print shows both kinds of artifact.\n\nA common mistake is every build hitting the public internet with no cache. Then npmjs being down blocks all deploys.", code: "repo = {\n    \"npm\": \"@org/ui@1.2.3\",\n    \"docker\": \"org/app@sha256:aaa\",\n}\nprint(repo)" },
    { id: 74, level: "intermediate", q: "semantic release / versioning?", a: "Semantic versions can come from commit types and a changelog. Libraries like this. Apps often version by git sha instead.\n\nfeat and fix commits tell the robot the bump. Do not lie in the version. Book editions versus a newspaper issue number.\n\nIn the code: commits are fix: timeout and feat: export csv. print says a library might bump minor for feat, an app might tag sha abc123.\n\nA common mistake is bumping a library version by mood. Then dependents cannot trust the number.", code: "commits = [\"fix: timeout\", \"feat: export csv\"]\nprint(\"library might bump minor for feat\")\nprint(\"app might tag sha abc123\")\n# teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 75, level: "beginner", q: "What is a release train?", a: "A train leaves on a timetable. Features that miss it wait for the next one. Useful for mobile stores.\n\nLess needed for web with true CD. Cadence reduces last-minute chaos. Still test each car. The platform does not wait for one late bag.\n\nIn the code: train leaves Wednesday, on_board feat A, missed feat B. print shows who caught the train.\n\nA common mistake is stuffing late features onto a train that already left testing. Then the timetable was theater.", code: "train = {\"leaves\": \"Wednesday\", \"on_board\": [\"feat A\"], \"missed\": [\"feat B\"]}\nprint(train)\n# teaching snippet: read the comments\n# this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 76, level: "intermediate", q: "DORA metrics?", a: "Deploy frequency, lead time, change fail rate, time to restore. They talk about performance without only vanity speed. Do not game them.\n\nUse them as a conversation, not a punishment dashboard. How often, how fast, how often it breaks, how fast you heal.\n\nIn the code: dora maps those four to daily, 2h, 8%, 20m. print shows the sports card.\n\nA common mistake is optimizing only deploy frequency. Tiny empty deploys can fake the metric.", code: "dora = {\n    \"deploy_freq\": \"daily\",\n    \"lead_time\": \"2h\",\n    \"fail_rate\": \"8%\",\n    \"mttr\": \"20m\",\n}\nprint(dora)" },
    { id: 77, level: "intermediate", q: "lead time vs cycle time?", a: "Lead time often means commit to production. Cycle time may mean ticket start to done. Say which clock you mean.\n\nInterviews mix these words. Draw the timeline. Wait from order versus wait from the kitchen starting.\n\nIn the code: clocks maps lead to commit -> prod and cycle to ticket start -> done. print shows both definitions.\n\nA common mistake is quoting a number without saying which clock. Then two teams argue about a word.", code: "clocks = {\n    \"lead\": \"commit -> prod\",\n    \"cycle\": \"ticket start -> done\",\n}\nprint(clocks)" },
    { id: 78, level: "intermediate", q: "toil?", a: "Toil is manual, repetitive, automatable work. Track it. Spend engineering time to delete it.\n\nIf on-call is only toil, people leave. Automate the top repeat pain. Leave the hard judgment to humans. Hand-washing 200 plates every night when a dishwasher exists.\n\nIn the code: toil lists restart box and renew cert by hand. print says automate the first item.\n\nA common mistake is hiring more people to do the same clicks. The pile grows; the clicks stay.", code: "toil = [\"restart box\", \"renew cert by hand\"]\nprint(\"automate\", toil[0])\n# teaching snippet: read the comments\n# this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 79, level: "intermediate", q: "platform engineering?", a: "A golden path: template repo, paved CI, default logs. Teams self-serve instead of each becoming a Kubernetes expert. The trail is the product.\n\nListen to app teams as customers. Too many options is not a platform. A well-marked hiking trail versus a jungle with a machete.\n\nIn the code: path maps template to service-starter, ci to reusable-workflow, obs to default dashboard. print shows the paved road.\n\nA common mistake is a platform with twenty ways to deploy. Then nobody shares a path.", code: "path = {\n    \"template\": \"service-starter\",\n    \"ci\": \"reusable-workflow\",\n    \"obs\": \"default dashboard\",\n}\nprint(path)" },
    { id: 80, level: "intermediate", q: "Inner loop vs outer loop?", a: "Inner loop is local run and test. Outer loop is CI, CD, and prod. A fast inner loop makes developers happy.\n\nThe outer loop protects users. Both need to exist. Do not make local so slow people skip tests. Practice at home versus the concert hall.\n\nIn the code: loops maps inner to save file -> see result and outer to PR -> CI -> prod. print shows both loops.\n\nA common mistake is CI that takes 40 minutes and no local test command. People push and hope.", code: "loops = {\n    \"inner\": \"save file -> see result\",\n    \"outer\": \"PR -> CI -> prod\",\n}\nprint(loops)" },
    { id: 81, level: "beginner", q: "Why Docker in DevOps?", a: "The artifact is an image. CI and prod run the same runtime box. It reduces works on my machine for the runtime.\n\nIt does not fix bad data or bad config by itself. Tag it by sha. The kitchen and the picnic open the same lunch box.\n\nIn the code: Dockerfile starts FROM node:20-alpine, WORKDIR /app, COPY package.json. Later you would COPY source and CMD start.\n\nA common mistake is a different Dockerfile for prod that is never tested in CI. Then the lunch box is not the same.", code: "# Dockerfile is the recipe for the box\nFROM node:20-alpine\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY package.json .\n# later COPY source and CMD start the app" },
    { id: 82, level: "intermediate", q: "why Kubernetes in DevOps?", a: "Kubernetes gives a standard API for deploy, scale, and self-heal. The cost is complexity. Small apps can live on a simpler PaaS.\n\nBe honest about team skill. You do not need an airport for one bus. A full airport versus a bus stop.\n\nIn the code: a Deployment wants replicas 3. The comment says the cluster keeps 3 ready pods.\n\nA common mistake is adopting Kubernetes for a single small app with no ops time. The platform becomes the product accidentally.", code: "# k8s desired state\napiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  # cluster keeps 3 ready pods" },
    { id: 83, level: "intermediate", q: "PaaS vs IaaS vs CaaS?", a: "IaaS: you get VMs. CaaS: you bring containers, like ECS or k8s. PaaS: you bring code, Heroku-style.\n\nPick based on control versus ops load. Interviews want the why. Renting land, renting a kitchen, or ordering a cooked meal.\n\nIn the code: models maps iaas to VMs, caas to containers, paas to code. print shows the three styles.\n\nA common mistake is picking IaaS because it feels more serious. You then patch VMs forever for no gain.", code: "models = {\n    \"iaas\": \"VMs\",\n    \"caas\": \"containers\",\n    \"paas\": \"code\",\n}\nprint(models)" },
    { id: 84, level: "intermediate", q: "finops?", a: "FinOps is watching cost: rightsize, idle boxes, storage tiers, tags. A spend spike is a DevOps alert too. Untagged resources are mystery meat.\n\nEngineers and finance pick savings plans together. Waste is also an ops bug. A shared kitchen bill with names on jars.\n\nIn the code: cost maps service api, env prod, idle False. print shows a tagged bill line. Tags make this bill readable.\n\nA common mistake is no tags. Then nobody knows which team owns the surprise bill.", code: "cost = {\"service\": \"api\", \"env\": \"prod\", \"idle\": False}\nprint(cost)\n# tags make this bill readable\n# teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 85, level: "intermediate", q: "tags on cloud resources?", a: "service, env, owner, cost-center. Bills and cleanup depend on them. Enforce with policy as code.\n\nProd versus dev must be obvious. No tag, no idea who to page. Name tags at a conference.\n\nIn the code: the S3 bucket photos has tags service photos, env prod, owner platform. Those labels travel with the resource.\n\nA common mistake is tagging by hand after create. New resources then ship naked until someone remembers.", code: "resource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"photos\" {\n  bucket = \"my-app-photos\"\n  tags = {\n    service = \"photos\"\n    env     = \"prod\"\n    owner   = \"platform\"\n  }\n}" },
    { id: 86, level: "advanced", q: "ephemeral preview environments?", a: "Each pull request gets a short-lived environment from IaC. Great for clicking the feature. Cost and fake data are the hard parts.\n\nTear down on merge. Use cheap sizes. Do not copy prod secrets blindly. A pop-up shop that vanishes when the sale ends.\n\nIn the code: preview maps pr 42, url pr-42.dev.example, ttl 2d. print shows that identity.\n\nA common mistake is leaving preview stacks forever. Then you pay for a graveyard of PR-12 from last year.", code: "# preview identity\npreview = {\"pr\": 42, \"url\": \"pr-42.dev.example\", \"ttl\": \"2d\"}\nprint(preview)\n# teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 87, level: "intermediate", q: "migrations of infra?", a: "Additive first: create new, dual-run, switch DNS, destroy old. Never in-place rewrite a prod database because the plan said so without a plan.\n\nDNS switch is a common last step. Have a rollback. State and data are the risk. New place ready, then boxes, then cancel the old lease.\n\nIn the code: steps is create new, dual run, switch dns, destroy old. join prints the path.\n\nA common mistake is destroy old first. Then you have nowhere to roll back.", code: "steps = [\"create new\", \"dual run\", \"switch dns\", \"destroy old\"]\nprint(\" -> \".join(steps))\n# teaching snippet: read the comments\n# this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 88, level: "intermediate", q: "state locking?", a: "Two writers corrupt Terraform state. Use a lock, like DynamoDB with S3, or Terraform Cloud. CI should serialize applies to the same workspace.\n\nWait for the lock. Two people editing one spreadsheet without file lock makes fiction. The catalog becomes a lie.\n\nIn the code: backend S3 uses bucket tf-state, key app.tfstate, dynamodb_table tf-locks. That table is the lock.\n\nA common mistake is local state on two laptops. Then both apply and the world is a coin flip.", code: "terraform {\n  backend \"s3\" {\n    bucket         = \"tf-state\"\n    key            = \"app.tfstate\"\n    dynamodb_table = \"tf-locks\"\n  }\n}" },
    { id: 89, level: "advanced", q: "workspaces vs directories?", a: "Workspaces are easy and easy to misuse. Separate folders or states per env is clearer for many teams. Be ready to discuss trade-offs.\n\nProd state should be hard to touch. Tabs mix up. Notebooks are heavier and safer. One notebook with tabs versus three notebooks.\n\nIn the code: comments show infra/dev/main.tf and infra/prod/main.tf. print says two states, two folders.\n\nA common mistake is one workspace name typo applying to prod. Folders make that harder.", code: "# directory layout idea\n# infra/dev/main.tf\n# infra/prod/main.tf\nprint(\"two states, two folders\")" },
    { id: 90, level: "intermediate", q: "module versioning?", a: "A floating module source can change prod on the next apply. Same lesson as npm latest. Pin versions.\n\nReview module upgrades like app upgrades. Read the changelog. 120g flour, not some flour.\n\nIn the code: module vpc source is org/vpc/aws with version 1.4.2. That pin is the safety.\n\nA common mistake is version = \">= 1.0\". The next apply can pull a breaking module.", code: "module \"vpc\" {\n  source  = \"org/vpc/aws\"\n  version = \"1.4.2\"\n}" },
    { id: 91, level: "beginner", q: "What is a runbook-driven deploy?", a: "Migrate, deploy, smoke, watch dashboards, announce. Even automated deploys need this the first few times. Then automate the card once it is boring.\n\nWrite it down. The robot does the flaps; the human still reads the card. A pilot's preflight list.\n\nIn the code: checklist is migrate, deploy, smoke, watch, announce. join prints that order.\n\nA common mistake is deploy with no announce. Then support learns from angry tickets.", code: "checklist = [\"migrate\", \"deploy\", \"smoke\", \"watch\", \"announce\"]\nprint(\" -> \".join(checklist))\n# teaching snippet: read the comments\n# this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 92, level: "intermediate", q: "chatops?", a: "People trigger status or deploys from Slack or Teams with an audit log. Convenient. Lock it down.\n\nAnyone in the channel should not be able to destroy prod. Auth and allowlists matter. Chat is not a secret store. An intercom only chefs can press.\n\nIn the code: msg has user ada and text deploy staging. console.log audit records that identity.\n\nA common mistake is a Slack bot with prod credentials and no user check. Then a joke command ships prod.", code: "// chat command becomes an API call with identity\nconst msg = { user: \"ada\", text: \"deploy staging\" };\nconsole.log(\"audit\", msg);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 93, level: "intermediate", q: "compliance as code?", a: "Policies in git: encryption on, public buckets off, allowed regions. This catches oops faster than a yearly PDF audit. Evidence can be the green policy job.\n\nStill need humans for gray areas. Start with the loudest risks. Spell-check for cloud rules.\n\nIn the code: policy has encrypt_at_rest True and public_s3 False. assert checks both, then print compliant.\n\nA common mistake is a 50-page PDF that nobody runs. Policy as code actually fails the PR.", code: "policy = {\"encrypt_at_rest\": True, \"public_s3\": False}\nassert policy[\"encrypt_at_rest\"] and not policy[\"public_s3\"]\nprint(\"compliant\")\n# teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 94, level: "intermediate", q: "change management vs CD?", a: "Classic CAB is a meeting. CD is merge-to-prod with a pipeline. Many orgs keep CAB for high risk and CD for standard changes.\n\nStandard changes are pre-approved pipelines. Describe both calmly. A fast lane with cameras versus a full interview at the border.\n\nIn the code: change type standard, path green pipeline, cab False. print shows a standard change that skips CAB.\n\nA common mistake is mocking all change meetings. Some changes really are high risk and need a pause.", code: "change = {\n    \"type\": \"standard\",\n    \"path\": \"green pipeline\",\n    \"cab\": False,\n}\nprint(change)" },
    { id: 95, level: "beginner", q: "What is a hotfix process?", a: "Branch from the released tag. Minimal change. Extra review. Fast pipeline. Then merge back to main so the fix is not lost.\n\nIf you forget main, the next release re-breaks prod. Keep the diff tiny. Write why in the commit. Repair the live set, then update the master drawings.\n\nIn the code: flow is tag v1.2.0, hotfix branch, ship, merge to main. join prints that path.\n\nA common mistake is hot-fixing prod and never merging to main. The next normal release undoes the fix.", code: "flow = [\"tag v1.2.0\", \"hotfix branch\", \"ship\", \"merge to main\"]\nprint(\" -> \".join(flow))\n# teaching snippet: read the comments\n# this is to understand the idea, not a command list" },
    { id: 96, level: "advanced", q: "incident during deploy?", a: "Stop more deploys. Roll back or forward quickly. Communicate. Freeze extra changes. A half-migrated database is the hard case.\n\nHave the expand/contract plan. One commander. Do not keep adding bolts. Stop the assembly line when a bolt is wrong.\n\nIn the code: play has stop_deploys True, choose rollback, comms status page. print shows that playbook.\n\nA common mistake is pushing 'one more fix' during the incident. Now you have two unknown states.", code: "play = {\n    \"stop_deploys\": True,\n    \"choose\": \"rollback\",\n    \"comms\": \"status page\",\n}\nprint(play)" },
    { id: 97, level: "intermediate", q: "load testing in the pipeline?", a: "You hit staging with a script of users. Do not DDoS production from CI. Use it before big launches, not on every PR unless it is cheap.\n\nWatch errors and latency. Keep the script in git. A dress rehearsal crowd, not a stampede in the real theater.\n\nIn the code: scenario has vus 20, duration 30s, target staging. console.log shows that k6-like idea.\n\nA common mistake is load testing production from a shared CI runner. You can take yourself down.", code: "// k6-like idea: virtual users\nconst scenario = { vus: 20, duration: \"30s\", target: \"staging\" };\nconsole.log(scenario);\n// teaching snippet: read the comments" },
    { id: 98, level: "intermediate", q: "synthetic monitoring?", a: "A script logs in every minute from the internet. It catches metrics green, checkout broken. Complements real-user monitoring.\n\nIf the shopper fails, page. Keep the script as close to a real user as you can. A mystery shopper on a schedule.\n\nIn the code: synthetic fetches https://shop.example/login and returns whether status is 200. The log says scripted user, not a real customer.\n\nA common mistake is only internal health checks. Then the public TLS cert can expire with green internal metrics.", code: "async function synthetic() {\n  const res = await fetch(\"https://shop.example/login\");\n  return res.status === 200;\n}\nconsole.log(\"scripted user, not a real customer\");" },
    { id: 99, level: "beginner", q: "What makes a good DevOps interview story?", a: "Tell a pipeline you built, a safer deploy, or a shorter incident. Numbers help: 2 hours to 15 minutes. Say what you would do next.\n\nOwn one slice deeply. Culture plus automation in one anecdote. A before-and-after photo, not a tool name-drop.\n\nIn the code: story maps before deploy 2h, after deploy 15m, how same artifact + smoke. print shows that anecdote shape.\n\nA common mistake is listing twenty tools with no story. Interviewers cannot tell what you actually did.", code: "story = {\n    \"before\": \"deploy 2h\",\n    \"after\": \"deploy 15m\",\n    \"how\": \"same artifact + smoke\",\n}\nprint(story)" },
    { id: 100, level: "advanced", q: "How do you design a delivery platform for a team?", a: "Trunk-based git, CI on PR, signed images, staging auto, prod gated. SLO dashboards, one-click rollback, a starter repo. Add GitOps when many services need drift control.\n\nStart thinner than a big-bang platform. Listen to the first two teams as customers. A small airport: runway, tower, fire truck, timetable.\n\nIn the code: platform is trunk + PR CI, signed image, staging auto, prod gated, slo + rollback. join prints that paved path.\n\nA common mistake is building a giant platform before one team ships on it. Then you built a ghost airport.", code: "platform = [\n    \"trunk + PR CI\",\n    \"signed image\",\n    \"staging auto\",\n    \"prod gated\",\n    \"slo + rollback\",\n]\nprint(\" -> \".join(platform))" }
  ]
};
